# Reproducibility, data and citation

This file follows SciLifeLab Serve's FAIR-application recommendations. These items support good research-software practice; they are not all container-runtime requirements.

## Code

- Keep a clear README with build/run instructions.
- Keep runtime versions reproducible. This starter pins Python packages exactly; Shiny projects should add `renv` when real R dependencies are introduced.
- Create versioned releases instead of relying only on a moving branch.
- The generated code uses the MIT license. Change it deliberately if your project needs another appropriate software license.

## Data

- Do not put sensitive/personal data on SciLifeLab Serve.
- Version the data used by each application release.
- Use an appropriate **data** license separately from the software license when you publish research data.
- For large or discipline-specific datasets, use an appropriate data repository and a persistent reference rather than treating GitHub or the Serve persistent volume as archival storage.

## Archival release

Serve's FAIR guide recommends depositing code and data in a repository that provides persistent identifiers and long-term availability; it gives Zenodo as a general example and describes GitHub–Zenodo release archiving.

## Citation

`CITATION.cff` is included as a small starting point. Replace the placeholder contributor information and add the software/data/article identifiers that users should cite.

Reference: https://serve.scilifelab.se/docs/application-hosting/fair/
